 "external_evidence": {
  "citation": "T. Mehtiyev, B. Tadesse, L. Arkoh, W. Assuncao. When Instruction Files Go Stale: Documentation Debt and Hidden Cost in Coding Agents. arXiv:2611.09802 (6 Nov 2026).",
  "url": "https://arxiv.org/abs/2611.09802",
  "summary": "22 maintenance tasks x 3 instruction versions (left stale / corrected / removed), 732 trajectories, two models, per-tool-call analysis. Stale test command: Claude Sonnet 5.5 needed ~2 extra tool calls per run (~1/3 more than corrected); the other two stale-guidance kinds showed no detectable difference; agents recovered from immediate failures but repaired no instruction file.",
  "relation": "independent concurrent evidence that stale instructions can add tool calls. Our in-harness result is consistent in direction (stale KB adds stale-path inspection actions: 6 post-KB stale touches vs 3 with the corrected KB) but small and fully recoverable in these three real-repo location cases (no wrong-path edits, no outcome effect), which calibrates how much of the 'hidden cost' translates to agentic harm."
 },
